"""Additional original language-boundary, prediction, and design practice."""
from cpp_lessons import unit, program, UNITS

S='03 Ownership, lifetime, and value semantics'
unit(S,'initializer-list-selection','Braces can select a different constructor',14,
     'List initialization has special rules for initializer_list constructors. A vector constructed with parentheses from count and value is different from a vector list-initialized from two element values.|This difference is an overload-resolution rule, not a preference for one punctuation style. State whether the interface is receiving a count, an element list, or a single domain value.|Check size and values when migrating initialization syntax. Replacing every parenthesis with braces can silently change behavior even when the program compiles cleanly.',
     program('vector','    std::vector<int> repeated(3, 7);\n    std::vector<int> listed{3, 7};\n    std::cout << repeated.size() << " " << listed.size() << "\\n";'),'3 2\n',
     ('Make both vectors contain the explicit element list 3, 7.', 'repeated(3, 7)', 'repeated{3, 7}', '2 2\n', 'The initializer-list constructor receives two elements in each declaration. Parentheses originally selected the count/value overload.'),
     'What does vector<int>{3,7} contain?', 'Two elements: 3 and 7', ['Three copies of 7','Seven copies of 3'], 'The initializer-list constructor is selected for the braced element list.', 'dcl.init.list')
unit(S,'guaranteed-elision','A prvalue can construct the result directly',17,
     'C++17 specifies direct construction for certain prvalue cases rather than requiring a temporary followed by a move. This permits some return-by-value code even when copying and moving are deleted.|Named return value optimization is a different case: returning a named local can depend on optional elision and still requires the relevant well-formed operations under the applicable rules.|Do not use log counts from one optimized build as a universal rule. Distinguish mandatory language behavior from optional optimizations and keep destructor accessibility requirements visible.',
     program('', '    auto value = make();\n    (void)value;', 'struct Item { Item() { std::cout << "constructed\\n"; } Item(const Item&) = delete; Item(Item&&) = delete; };\nItem make() { return Item{}; }\n'),'constructed\n',
     ('Construct the value directly from a prvalue without calling the factory.', 'auto value = make();', 'auto value = Item{};', 'constructed\n', 'Both forms directly construct the destination object under the selected C++17 rules. No accessible move constructor is needed for this case.'),
     'Is returning a named local the same mandatory-elision case as return Item{}?', 'No; named return value optimization is a separate case', ['Yes, always','Only with a virtual destructor'], 'Optional NRVO must be distinguished from specified prvalue construction.', 'class.copy.elision')
unit(S,'reference-qualified-members','Member ref qualifiers distinguish borrowing from consumption',14,
     'A member function can be qualified with & or && to constrain the object expression category on which it is called. This supports interfaces that borrow from lvalues and consume rvalues deliberately.|A named object remains an lvalue expression even if its type involved an rvalue reference. std::move explicitly changes the expression category for overload selection.|Returning references from a temporary receiver can dangle. Use a value-returning rvalue overload when transferring a result out of a temporary is the intended contract.',
     program('utility','    Box b;\n    std::cout << b.category() << " " << std::move(b).category() << "\\n";', 'struct Box { const char* category() & { return "borrow"; } const char* category() && { return "consume"; } };\n'),'borrow consume\n',
     ('Call the lvalue-qualified overload twice without consuming the receiver.', 'std::move(b).category()', 'b.category()', 'borrow borrow\n', 'The receiver expression b is an lvalue on both calls. The && overload is not selected merely because the implementation might later destroy b.'),
     'What selects the &&-qualified overload in this example?', 'An rvalue object expression produced by std::move(b)', ['The variable name b','The constness of the return string'], 'Ref qualification participates in selecting a member for the implicit object argument.', 'class.mfct.non.static')

S='04 Objects, interfaces, and software design'
D=[]
unit(S,'hidden-friends','ADL finds operations associated with an argument type',14,
     'Argument-dependent lookup considers associated namespaces and classes in certain unqualified calls. A hidden friend can make a nonmember operation available through its class argument without adding a broadly visible ordinary declaration.|This pattern is useful for symmetric operators whose operands include the domain type. It does not mean every arbitrary unqualified name is searched everywhere.|Understand ordinary lookup, ADL, and overload resolution as separate steps. Unexpected implicit conversions or generic overloads can still make an API confusing.',
     program('', '    std::cout << (Token{3} == Token{3}) << "\\n";', 'struct Token { int value; friend bool operator==(Token a, Token b) { return a.value == b.value; } };\n'),'1\n',
     ('Compare unequal domain values through the same hidden friend.', 'Token{3} == Token{3}', 'Token{3} == Token{4}', '0\n', 'The Token operands associate the friend operation with lookup, and the comparison then checks their stored values.'),
     'Why can this nonmember equality operator be found?', 'Argument-dependent lookup associates it with Token', ['Every friend is a virtual member','The linker guesses its name'], 'A hidden friend can participate in ADL through the associated class.', 'basic.lookup.argdep',D)
unit(S,'prototype-cloning','A clone operation needs a deep-copy ownership policy',14,
     'A polymorphic clone returns an independently owned object while preserving the relevant dynamic type. It must define how owned resources and shared dependencies are copied.|Returning unique_ptr<Base> makes release ownership explicit when the base has a virtual destructor. Copying a raw pointer field alone may accidentally share mutable state or double-delete an allocation.|Test that modifying the clone leaves the original independent where that is the contract. A shared cache or external service may intentionally remain shared and needs separate documentation.',
     program('memory string', '    Label original("cat");\n    auto copy = original.clone();\n    std::cout << copy->text() << "\\n";', 'struct View { virtual ~View() = default; virtual std::unique_ptr<View> clone() const = 0; virtual std::string text() const = 0; };\nstruct Label : View { std::string value; explicit Label(std::string s) : value(std::move(s)) {} std::unique_ptr<View> clone() const override { return std::make_unique<Label>(*this); } std::string text() const override { return value; } };\n'),'cat\n',
     ('Modify the original after cloning and inspect the clone again.', 'std::cout << copy->text()', 'original.value = "dog";\n    std::cout << copy->text()', 'cat\n', 'The copied std::string is an independent owned value. The clone remains a Label behind the View ownership interface.'),
     'What should a polymorphic clone specify?', 'Ownership, dynamic type, and copying policy for contained resources', ['Only the method name','That every dependency must be shared'], 'Cloning is a resource and value-semantics contract, not just copying an address.', 'class.virtual',D)
unit(S,'queued-command-lifetime','Queued commands must survive or reject target loss',17,
     'A command stores an action for later execution. Captured inputs and target references need a lifetime policy because execution can occur after the original scope has ended.|A weak_ptr target can let a command reject work when its target is gone. lock obtains temporary strong ownership only when the object is still alive; it does not synchronize mutable object data.|Retries and undo need additional semantics. Repeating an external side effect is not automatically safe merely because an action fits inside a callable wrapper.',
     program('functional memory', '    std::function<bool()> command;\n    { auto target = std::make_shared<int>(7); std::weak_ptr<int> weak = target; command = [weak] { auto live = weak.lock(); return live && *live == 7; }; }\n    std::cout << command() << "\\n";'),'0\n',
     ('Capture ownership deliberately so the queued command keeps its immutable target alive.', [('std::weak_ptr<int> weak = target;', 'auto weak = target;'), ('auto live = weak.lock();', 'auto live = weak;')], '', '1\n', 'The callable now stores a shared owning handle. This changes the target lifetime contract; it is appropriate only when retaining that target is intended.'),
     'Does capturing a raw reference ensure a queued target survives?', 'No', ['Always','Only for std::function'], 'Delayed invocation requires a deliberate ownership or availability check.', 'func.wrap.func',D)
unit(S,'adapter-and-bridge','Adapt an old interface without hiding its dependency',14,
     'An adapter translates an existing interface to the contract a client expects. A bridge separates an abstraction from an independently varying implementation; the patterns solve different dependency problems.|Composition keeps the legacy object behind a narrow wrapper without requiring the client to inherit from its concrete type. The adapter must translate failure and ownership behavior too.|Do not silently retain a borrowed legacy object beyond its lifetime. Store ownership or a reference according to the intended relationship and state which party must outlive the wrapper.',
     program('', '    Legacy old; Adapter current(old);\n    std::cout << current.read() << "\\n";', 'struct Legacy { int old_read() const { return 7; } };\nstruct Adapter { const Legacy& target; explicit Adapter(const Legacy& value) : target(value) {} int read() const { return target.old_read(); } };\n'),'7\n',
     ('Change the adapter into an owning snapshot of the small legacy value.', 'const Legacy& target;', 'Legacy target;', '7\n', 'The wrapper now copies Legacy rather than borrowing it. For a real noncopyable foreign resource, ownership would need an explicit owning handle instead.'),
     'What must outlive the original reference-based adapter?', 'The borrowed Legacy object', ['Only the read call parameter','Nothing because references own targets'], 'Composition through a reference does not extend the target lifetime.', 'class',D)

S='05 Generic and compile-time programming'
unit(S,'dependent-names','Dependent types need explicit parsing guidance',14,
     'A name dependent on a template parameter may not be known as a type during initial template parsing. typename can identify a dependent qualified type name, and template disambiguates a dependent member template in the relevant syntax.|Two-phase lookup separates information available at template definition from information checked during instantiation. An unqualified base member in a dependent base may require this-> or another deliberate qualification.|Do not fix every template diagnostic by adding keywords blindly. Identify whether the name is a type, template, value, or overload set and which lookup phase applies.',
     program('vector', '    std::vector<int> values{2, 4};\n    std::cout << first_or_zero(values) << "\\n";', 'template<class Container> typename Container::value_type first_or_zero(const Container& values) { return values.empty() ? typename Container::value_type{} : values.front(); }\n'),'2\n',
     ('Exercise the empty-container value-initialization branch.', 'values{2, 4}', 'values{}', '0\n', 'The dependent value_type names int for this instantiation. Empty braces value-initialize that result to zero.'),
     'What does typename communicate in Container::value_type?', 'That the dependent qualified name denotes a type', ['That the type is dynamically allocated','That lookup can be skipped'], 'It disambiguates parsing while later instantiation verifies the actual member type.', 'temp.res')
unit(S,'detection-idiom','Detect an expression in a substitution context',17,
     'void_t can support a detection trait by making an expression part of substitution. A partial specialization is selected only when the expression is well-formed in that context.|Detection answers a syntactic question. A size() member might have unexpected complexity, side effects, or return semantics even when the expression exists.|Prefer a named concept in C++20 when it better expresses the contract, while understanding detection traits when maintaining C++17 interfaces.',
     program('type_traits utility vector', '    std::cout << HasSize<int>::value << " " << HasSize<std::vector<int>>::value << "\\n";', 'template<class T, class = void> struct HasSize : std::false_type {};\ntemplate<class T> struct HasSize<T, std::void_t<decltype(std::declval<const T&>().size())>> : std::true_type {};\n'),'0 1\n',
     ('Detect const size access on a type that only supplies a non-const overload.', [('std::vector<int>>::value', 'MutableOnly>::value'), ('template<class T, class = void>', 'struct MutableOnly { int size() { return 1; } };\ntemplate<class T, class = void>')], '', '0 0\n', 'The trait explicitly asks for size on const T&, so a non-const-only member does not satisfy the expression.'),
     'Does detecting size() prove its complexity is constant?', 'No', ['Yes','Only when void_t is used'], 'Expression validity does not prove a semantic performance contract.', 'meta.trans.other')
unit(S,'crtp','Static polymorphism trades runtime openness for type coupling',14,
     'The curiously recurring template pattern passes the derived type as a template argument to its base. It can share an implementation that calls derived operations without virtual dispatch.|The cast from the base to the declared derived type depends on the inheritance design being correct. A mismatched instantiation can invalidate that assumption.|Use static polymorphism when compile-time composition fits the extension boundary. It does not replace a runtime plugin interface or automatically guarantee better measured performance.',
     program('', '    Sensor sensor;\n    std::cout << sensor.doubled() << "\\n";', 'template<class Derived> struct Measurement { int doubled() const { return static_cast<const Derived&>(*this).read() * 2; } };\nstruct Sensor : Measurement<Sensor> { int read() const { return 7; } };\n'),'14\n',
     ('Change the derived measurement without changing the reusable algorithm.', 'return 7;', 'return 9;', '18\n', 'The base algorithm is instantiated against Sensor and invokes its statically selected read operation.'),
     'Does this pattern require virtual dispatch?', 'No', ['Always','Only for const functions'], 'The derived type is supplied as a template argument and selected statically.', 'temp',D)
unit(S,'invoke','Invoke unifies callable and member-pointer forms',17,
     'std::invoke provides a uniform interface for function objects, function pointers, and supported member pointers. Member-function invocation still needs an appropriate receiver.|A member pointer is not an ordinary function pointer. The receiver type, cv qualification, and reference category still participate in validity.|Generic callable wrappers must preserve argument and return lifetimes. Returning a reference obtained through invoke does not extend the receiver lifetime.',
     program('functional', '    Counter c{7};\n    std::cout << std::invoke(&Counter::add, c, 3) << "\\n";', 'struct Counter { int value; int add(int n) const { return value + n; } };\n'),'10\n',
     ('Invoke a pointer to the data member instead.', 'std::invoke(&Counter::add, c, 3)', 'std::invoke(&Counter::value, c)', '7\n', 'invoke can access the member through a suitable object. This expression refers to the existing member rather than allocating a new Counter.'),
     'Does a pointer-to-member function include a complete receiver object?', 'No; an appropriate receiver must be supplied', ['Yes','Only for const members'], 'A member pointer identifies a member operation, not the lifetime of a target instance.', 'func.invoke')

S='06 Collections, algorithms, and ranges'
unit(S,'sets-and-duplicates','Equivalent keys control duplicate retention',14,
     'set keeps one element per comparator-equivalence class; multiset can retain multiple equivalent elements. Equivalence is defined by the ordering relation, not necessarily operator==.|For hashed containers, equality and hashing must agree so equivalent keys produce the required compatible hash values. Modifying an indexed key through an unsupported route can break lookup invariants.|Choose a container from duplicate and ordering requirements. Do not discard records silently when their business identity is finer than the selected key.',
     program('set', '    std::set<int> unique{2, 2, 3}; std::multiset<int> repeated{2, 2, 3};\n    std::cout << unique.count(2) << " " << repeated.count(2) << "\\n";'),'1 2\n',
     ('Insert a third equivalent value into each container.', 'std::cout << unique.count(2)', 'unique.insert(2); repeated.insert(2);\n    std::cout << unique.count(2)', '1 3\n', 'The set still retains one representative, while the multiset retains another equivalent element.'),
     'Can multiset retain several comparator-equivalent keys?', 'Yes', ['No','Only if operator== returns false'], 'Uniqueness policy and comparator equivalence determine retention.', 'associative.reqmts')
unit(S,'priority-queues','A priority queue exposes one selected extreme',14,
     'priority_queue is a container adaptor organized according to a comparator. Its default ordering exposes a maximum element at top, not the earliest inserted element.|push and pop maintain heap organization; top observes the selected element. pop does not return that value, so read it before removal when needed.|Do not assume stable order among equivalent priorities. Add an explicit sequence key if deterministic FIFO behavior for equal priorities is part of the requirement.',
     program('queue vector functional', '    std::priority_queue<int> work;\n    for (int n : {3, 1, 7}) work.push(n);\n    std::cout << work.top() << "\\n";'),'7\n',
     ('Use a minimum-priority ordering instead.', 'std::priority_queue<int> work;', 'std::priority_queue<int, std::vector<int>, std::greater<int>> work;', '1\n', 'The greater comparator reverses which extreme appears at top. Insertion order is still not the priority rule.'),
     'Does priority_queue::pop return the removed value?', 'No; inspect top before popping', ['Yes','Only for int'], 'The adaptor separates observation and removal operations.', 'priqueue')

S='07 Standard-library vocabulary and systems facilities'
unit(S,'stream-format-state','Some formatting state persists and some is consumed',14,
     'Stream manipulators change formatting state. Numeric base and fill typically persist, while width applies to the next relevant formatted insertion and is then reset under its rules.|A reusable formatting helper should preserve caller state when its contract promises not to affect subsequent output. Use a scoped policy rather than guessing which flags changed.|Keep presentation formatting separate from a stable serialization format. Locale, rounding, and stream state can otherwise change a supposedly machine-readable output.',
     program('iomanip', '    std::cout << std::setfill(\'0\') << std::setw(3) << 7 << " " << 8 << "\\n";'),'007 8\n',
     ('Apply a width to the second integer as well.', '<< " " << 8', '<< " " << std::setw(3) << 8', '007 008\n', 'The fill remains zero, but width must be specified again before the second integer insertion.'),
     'Does setw(3) normally persist for every later integer insertion?', 'No', ['Yes','Only with setfill'], 'Width is consumed by the relevant formatted insertion rather than acting like every persistent stream flag.', 'ostream.formatted.reqmts')
unit(S,'complex-numbers','Complex values supply algebra with floating limits',14,
     'complex<T> represents real and imaginary components and supplies complex arithmetic. Its magnitude is different from either component and from the sum of component magnitudes.|The component arithmetic has the numeric limitations of T and the specified library operations. Algebraic identities over exact complex numbers need justified tolerances when using floating representations.|Use a stable mathematical contract and test special values where relevant. An exact result in one small integer-valued example does not establish exactness for every floating calculation.',
     program('complex', '    std::complex<double> z(3, 4);\n    std::cout << std::abs(z) << " " << std::norm(z) << "\\n";'),'5 25\n',
     ('Conjugate the value before measuring it.', 'std::abs(z) << " " << std::norm(z)', 'std::abs(std::conj(z)) << " " << std::norm(std::conj(z))', '5 25\n', 'Conjugation changes the imaginary sign while preserving magnitude and squared magnitude. The chosen values have simple exact expected results.'),
     'What does norm return for a complex number?', 'Its squared magnitude', ['Only the imaginary part','Always the same value as abs'], 'abs gives magnitude and norm gives the sum of squared component magnitudes under its contract.', 'complex')
unit(S,'calendar-dates','Calendar validity is separate from field construction',20,
     'chrono calendar types represent civil date fields and allow a validity query. Constructing a year_month_day does not mean the combination describes a real calendar date.|Leap years and month lengths require calendar rules. Use ok to validate the combined fields before interpreting an external date as valid.|A civil date is not yet a complete instant with time zone and time-of-day semantics. Time-zone database support and deployment availability require separate checks.',
     program('chrono', '    using namespace std::chrono;\n    year_month_day date{year{2024}, month{2}, day{29}};\n    std::cout << date.ok() << "\\n";'),'1\n',
     ('Check the corresponding non-leap-year date.', 'year{2024}', 'year{2023}', '0\n', 'February 29 is invalid in 2023. Field construction and full date validation are separate steps.'),
     'Does constructing year_month_day always establish a valid date?', 'No; check the combined fields with ok()', ['Yes','Only for February'], 'The representation can hold field combinations that fail calendar validation.', 'time.cal')
unit(S,'utf8-code-units','UTF-8 literals expose code units rather than graphemes',20,
     'C++20 UTF-8 string literals use char8_t code units. A code unit is not necessarily an entire Unicode scalar value, and a scalar value is not necessarily a user-perceived grapheme.|The literal in this example encodes one non-ASCII scalar using two UTF-8 code units plus a null terminator. sizeof observes storage, not a general character count.|Do not slice external UTF-8 text at arbitrary byte positions. Validate encoding, decode at scalar boundaries when needed, and use a suitable Unicode layer for normalization and grapheme handling.',
     program('', '    constexpr auto& text = u8"\u00e9";\n    std::cout << sizeof(text) << "\\n";'),'3\n',
     ('Compare the storage of a single ASCII UTF-8 literal.', 'u8"\u00e9"', 'u8"A"', '2\n', 'The ASCII literal uses one code unit plus the null terminator. sizeof remains a storage query.'),
     'Does sizeof a UTF-8 literal directly report grapheme count?', 'No', ['Yes','Only when char8_t is enabled'], 'Encoded storage units and user-perceived characters are different levels.', 'lex.string')
unit(S,'bit-cast','A representation copy is not arbitrary type punning',20,
     'bit_cast returns a value whose object representation is obtained from a suitably constrained source of the same size. The source and destination must satisfy the required trivial-copy properties.|The existence of a representation does not guarantee every destination bit pattern represents a usable value. Padding and indeterminate bits need the applicable standard rules.|Prefer bit_cast over violating aliasing through an unrelated reference when a legitimate representation operation is intended. Still define a wire format separately.',
     program('bit cstdint', '    const std::uint32_t bits = 7;\n    auto copy = std::bit_cast<std::uint32_t>(bits);\n    std::cout << copy << "\\n";'),'7\n',
     ('Check round-trip copying through a same-size unsigned representation.', 'auto copy = std::bit_cast<std::uint32_t>(bits);', 'auto bytes = std::bit_cast<std::array<std::byte, sizeof(bits)>>(bits);\n    auto copy = std::bit_cast<std::uint32_t>(bytes);', '7\n', 'The example round-trips an initialized unsigned integer representation without assuming a particular byte order. The answer needs explicit array and cstddef headers supplied with the lesson.'),
     'Does bit_cast define a portable serialized byte order?', 'No', ['Yes','Only for unsigned integers'], 'A representation copy retains implementation representation; serialization needs an external format.', 'bit.cast')
UNITS[-1]['code']='#include <array>\n#include <cstddef>\n'+UNITS[-1]['code']
UNITS[-1]['solution']='#include <array>\n#include <cstddef>\n'+UNITS[-1]['solution']

S='08 Concurrency, synchronization, and coroutines'
unit(S,'semaphores','A semaphore coordinates permits without owning payloads',20,
     'A counting semaphore tracks available permits and can coordinate bounded access or readiness. It does not itself store the protected payload or extend any payload lifetime.|A release/acquire handoff can coordinate the surrounding protocol under the specified synchronization rules. Match permit accounting to actual work and shutdown responsibilities.|A missing release can leave a waiter blocked indefinitely. Teardown must make outstanding waiters complete before the semaphore and dependencies are destroyed.',
     program('semaphore thread', '    std::binary_semaphore ready(0); int value = 0;\n    std::thread producer([&] { value = 7; ready.release(); });\n    ready.acquire(); std::cout << value << "\\n"; producer.join();'),'7\n',
     ('Publish a different one-shot payload while retaining the handoff.', 'value = 7;', 'value = 9;', '9\n', 'The semaphore handoff precedes the payload read, and join completes the thread lifetime before local state is destroyed.'),
     'Does the semaphore own the int payload?', 'No', ['Yes','Only with binary_semaphore'], 'Permit synchronization and payload ownership remain separate responsibilities.', 'thread.sema',[])
unit(S,'atomic-ref','atomic_ref requires stable suitable backing storage',20,
     'atomic_ref permits atomic operations on an existing suitably aligned object while its requirements hold. The object lifetime and required alignment are still external responsibilities.|While an object is accessed through atomic_ref, follow the required atomic-access discipline rather than mixing conflicting ordinary accesses. The wrapper does not convert all other aliases into atomics.|Use the required_alignment constant for backing storage and keep referenced objects alive through every atomic_ref use. This is an interoperability tool, not a general aliasing escape hatch.',
     program('atomic', '    alignas(std::atomic_ref<int>::required_alignment) int value = 0;\n    { std::atomic_ref<int> ref(value); ref.fetch_add(3); std::cout << ref.load() << "\\n"; }'),'3\n',
     ('Add two atomic increments through the same valid reference.', 'ref.fetch_add(3);', 'ref.fetch_add(3); ref.fetch_add(4);', '7\n', 'Both operations use the atomic reference while its required backing storage remains live and aligned.'),
     'Does atomic_ref extend the referenced object lifetime?', 'No', ['Yes','Only for int'], 'The backing object remains owned elsewhere and must satisfy alignment and lifetime requirements.', 'atomics.ref.generic',[])

# Prediction problems retain full programs and explicitly ask about their output.
PREDICTIONS={
    'initializer-list-selection':'Parentheses select the count/value constructor with size three; braces select the two-element initializer list.',
    'reference-qualified-members':'The named receiver is an lvalue, while std::move supplies an rvalue receiver expression.',
    'hidden-friends':'ADL finds the equality friend and both stored integers equal three.',
    'dependent-names':'The nonempty vector starts with two; the dependent value_type resolves to int.',
    'detection-idiom':'int has no size member, while const vector<int> provides a size expression.',
    'sets-and-duplicates':'set retains one representative of key two, while multiset retains both equivalent occurrences.',
    'priority-queues':'The default priority queue exposes its largest stored value, seven.',
    'stream-format-state':'Width three is consumed by the first integer; zero fill persists but no width remains for the second integer.',
    'complex-numbers':'Magnitude is sqrt(3*3+4*4)=5; norm is the squared magnitude 25.',
    'calendar-dates':'2024 is a leap year, so February 29 passes the combined calendar validity check.',
    'utf8-code-units':'This UTF-8 literal stores two char8_t code units for the scalar plus one null terminator.'
}
for entry in UNITS:
    if entry['key'] in PREDICTIONS:
        entry['kind']='Predict output'
        entry['question']='What is the exact stdout of the displayed base program?'
        entry['correct']=repr(entry['expectedStdout'])
        entry['wrong']=['No output is produced.','The program is ill-formed in its stated standard.']
        entry['reasoning']='The stdout is '+repr(entry['expectedStdout'])+'. '+PREDICTIONS[entry['key']]

S='04 Objects, interfaces, and software design'
unit(S,'default-arguments','Default arguments follow the static declaration',14,
     'Default arguments are supplied at the call site according to the visible declaration. A virtual override can change the called function without changing the default argument selected through a base interface.|This combination can surprise callers: the dynamic function body may receive a default from the static base type. Avoid giving overrides inconsistent defaults when that ambiguity is not intentional.|Prefer one nonvirtual front door with a stable default that delegates to a virtual implementation, or require an explicit argument at the polymorphic boundary.',
     program('', '    Derived d; Base& base = d;\n    std::cout << base.value() << " " << d.value() << "\\n";', 'struct Base { virtual int value(int n = 3) const { return n; } virtual ~Base() = default; };\nstruct Derived : Base { int value(int n = 5) const override { return n * 10; } };\n'),'30 50\n',
     ('Supply the same explicit argument through both interfaces.', [('base.value()', 'base.value(7)'), ('d.value()', 'd.value(7)')], '', '70 70\n', 'Both calls dispatch to Derived::value with the explicitly supplied seven. No default argument is needed.'),
     'Which declaration supplies the default when calling through Base&?', 'The visible Base declaration', ['Always the dynamic Derived declaration','Whichever default is numerically larger'], 'Default-argument substitution is a call-site rule separate from virtual dispatch.', 'dcl.fct.default')

S='05 Generic and compile-time programming'
unit(S,'class-template-deduction','Deduction guides select a class-template specialization',17,
     'Class-template argument deduction can infer template arguments from an initializer. Deduction guides add declarations describing that inference; they do not execute a constructor body themselves.|The deduced specialization must still be constructible from the supplied arguments. A guide can intentionally deduce an owned string instead of a borrowed character pointer.|Review ownership and initializer_list interactions when adding guides. Convenient deduction should not silently change which object owns external storage.',
     program('string type_traits utility', '    Box first(7); Box second("cat");\n    std::cout << std::is_same_v<decltype(first), Box<int>> << " " << std::is_same_v<decltype(second), Box<std::string>> << "\\n";', 'template<class T> struct Box { T value; explicit Box(T v) : value(std::move(v)) {} };\nBox(const char*) -> Box<std::string>;\n'),'1 1\n',
     ('Construct the second box from an explicit std::string while retaining the same owned type.', 'Box second("cat")', 'Box second(std::string("cat"))', '1 1\n', 'The constructor-based deduction now supplies Box<string> directly. The guide handled the character-pointer spelling in the original.'),
     'Does a deduction guide itself allocate or execute a constructor body?', 'No; it participates in selecting template arguments', ['Always','Only when the guide returns string'], 'Construction occurs after the specialization has been deduced.', 'over.match.class.deduct')
unit(S,'initializer-list-lifetime','initializer_list is a view of a backing array',14,
     'initializer_list provides read-only access to a compiler-created backing array under its lifetime rules. Copying the initializer_list wrapper does not copy the elements into independent owned storage.|A local initializer_list initialized by a braced list can keep its backing array live for that local use. Returning a wrapper for a new local backing array does not make that array survive the return.|Use an owning container for a returned sequence. Const elements also constrain attempts to move resources out of initializer-list construction.',
     program('initializer_list numeric vector', '    std::initializer_list<int> values{2, 3, 4};\n    auto view = values;\n    std::cout << std::accumulate(view.begin(), view.end(), 0) << "\\n";'),'9\n',
     ('Make the copied range an owning vector instead of another list view.', 'auto view = values;', 'std::vector<int> view(values);', '9\n', 'The vector constructs its own elements from the live initializer list. It remains an owner independently of the wrapper lifetime.'),
     'Does copying an initializer_list wrapper copy its backing elements?', 'No', ['Always','Only when the list has three elements'], 'The wrapper refers to the backing array and does not become its own independent container.', 'dcl.init.list')

S='09 Toolchains, evolution, and expert boundaries'
unit(S,'preprocessor','Macros transform tokens before type checking',14,
     'The preprocessor expands tokens before ordinary C++ compilation. A function-like macro can duplicate an argument expression, ignore type boundaries, or collide with an unrelated identifier.|Parenthesizing macro parameters fixes some precedence problems but does not prevent repeated evaluation. Prefer a constexpr function or template when typed single evaluation is the desired contract.|Do not run a repeated-side-effect macro as a portable prediction exercise. Some such expressions have undefined or version-sensitive behavior; reason about expansion and replace the unsafe interface.',
     program('', '    std::cout << SQUARE(1 + 2) << "\\n";', '#define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))\n'),'9\n',
     ('Replace the macro with a typed single-evaluation function template.', '#define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))', 'template<class T> constexpr T SQUARE(T x) { return x * x; }', '9\n', 'The argument is evaluated to initialize one function parameter. The function body then uses that stored value twice rather than evaluating the caller expression twice.'),
     'Do parentheses around macro arguments prevent repeated evaluation?', 'No', ['Yes','Only for integers'], 'Parentheses affect grouping while the token expansion can still duplicate an argument.', 'cpp.replace')
unit(S,'namespace-lookup','Qualification selects the intended declaration',14,
     'Namespaces organize declarations and give qualified names. A namespace alias supplies a shorter spelling without copying the declarations or creating a different namespace.|using directives affect unqualified lookup and can make names ambiguous. Avoid exposing broad using directives from a public header when they would change client lookup unexpectedly.|An anonymous namespace gives translation-unit-local entities under its rules. Namespaces alone do not define an ABI versioning or symbol-visibility policy.',
     program('', '    std::cout << chosen::read() << " " << second::read() << "\\n";', 'namespace first { int read() { return 3; } }\nnamespace second { int read() { return 7; } }\nnamespace chosen = first;\n'),'3 7\n',
     ('Point the namespace alias at the second namespace.', 'namespace chosen = first;', 'namespace chosen = second;', '7 7\n', 'The alias changes lookup spelling; it does not modify either read definition.'),
     'Does a namespace alias copy its target namespace declarations?', 'No; it gives another spelling for the namespace', ['Yes','Only for functions'], 'Aliases name an existing namespace rather than duplicating its entities.', 'namespace.alias')
unit(S,'manual-union-lifetime','A union needs an active-member lifetime policy',20,
     'A union provides overlapping storage for alternatives, but a general nontrivial member lifetime is not automatically managed like variant. Construction and destruction must match the active member.|construct_at and destroy_at can express explicit lifetime operations on suitable storage. Taking a member address does not mean a string object is already live there.|Prefer variant when its closed-alternative semantics fit. A production manual union wrapper needs a tag, exception policy, and correct special members; the minimal demonstration intentionally manages one known transition only.',
     program('memory string', '    Storage storage;\n    std::construct_at(&storage.text, "cat");\n    std::cout << storage.text << "\\n";\n    std::destroy_at(&storage.text);', 'union Storage { int number; std::string text; Storage() : number(0) {} ~Storage() {} };\n'),'cat\n',
     ('After destroying the string, construct and read the integer member.', 'std::destroy_at(&storage.text);', 'std::destroy_at(&storage.text);\n    std::construct_at(&storage.number, 9);\n    std::cout << storage.number << "\\n";', 'cat\n9\n', 'The active string is destroyed before the integer lifetime is started. The trivial integer needs no custom destruction operation in this final state.'),
     'Does an empty user-written union destructor destroy whichever nontrivial member is active?', 'No; the wrapper must manage that policy', ['Always','Only for std::string'], 'A manual union is not an automatically tagged variant.', 'class.union')
unit(S,'custom-deleter','A unique owner can carry the correct release function',14,
     'A resource-specific deleter can pair unique ownership with the release operation required by a resource. The deleter is part of the owning type and may carry state whose lifetime also needs review.|Use the allocation and release family consistently. A pointer from malloc requires a free-compatible release contract, while an object created by new normally requires the matching delete path.|The worked example counts one custom delete on scope exit. Such counts cover the exercised ownership paths; they are not a process-wide heap profile.',
     program('memory', '    int released = 0;\n    { std::unique_ptr<int, Release> value(new int(7), Release{&released}); std::cout << *value << "\\n"; }\n    std::cout << released << "\\n";', 'struct Release { int* count; void operator()(int* p) const noexcept { delete p; ++*count; } };\n'),'7\n1\n',
     ('Move the custom owner and verify only the moved-to owner releases.', 'std::cout << *value << "\\n";', 'auto next = std::move(value); std::cout << *next << "\\n";', '7\n1\n', 'Moving transfers both the pointer and deleter state. The counter outlives both owner handles and records a single actual deletion.'),
     'What must a custom deleter match?', 'The resource allocation and release contract', ['Only the pointer variable name','The size of the enclosing class'], 'Ownership is correct only when release is appropriate for the acquired resource.', 'unique.ptr.single')

# The demonstration's manually constructed member also needs exception-path cleanup.
for entry in UNITS:
    if entry['key'] == 'manual-union-lifetime':
        for field in ('code','solution'):
            entry[field]=entry[field].replace('std::construct_at(&storage.text, "cat");',
                'std::construct_at(&storage.text, "cat");\n    auto life = std::unique_ptr<std::string, void(*)(std::string*)>(&storage.text, [](std::string* p) { std::destroy_at(p); });')
            entry[field]=entry[field].replace('std::destroy_at(&storage.text);','life.reset();')
